#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100642 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbb4e0a0-55ec-5cc7-9617-99cec8108940" :question/math-academy-id "e-540" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 5y \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 5 \\\\\n\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= 5\\int dx \\\\\n\\int \\frac{1}{y}dy &= 5\\int dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid y \\mid &= 5x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\pm  e^{5x + C} \\\\\n&= \\pm  e^{C}e^{5x} \\\\\n&= Ke^{5x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= 3 \\\\\nKe^{5 \\cdot 0} &= 3 \\\\\nK &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x} \\,\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891e9004-961b-539a-ab93-0752347fd077" :question/math-academy-id "e-541" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$. Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (3y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\sin (2x) \\\\\n\\int \\cos (3y)\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= - \\int \\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n\\int \\cos (3y)dy &= - \\int \\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}\\sin (3y) &= \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2x) + C \\\\\n\\sin (3y) &= \\frac{3}{2}\\cos (2x) + C_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C$.\n\nNow let's apply the condition\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nto find $C_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin (3 \\cdot 0) &= \\frac{3}{2}\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) + C_{1} \\\\\n\\sin (0) &= \\frac{3}{2}\\cos (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) + C_{1} \\\\\n0 &= 0 + C_{1} \\\\\nC_{1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y) = \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fa13493-411e-5b0f-83c5-a1c4a3c4c5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{3} = x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099451} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cfa395-8414-50b1-8224-cb9e8d291a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{3} = \\frac{1}{3}x^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099452} {:answer/id #uuid "d8996977-d1af-5583-8f78-5ac2870cf12c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{3} = 3x^{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099453} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7d3a5a-6085-5ac7-984b-91d90200a4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{3} = x^{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099454} {:answer/id #uuid "2b0b73fb-ed0c-52e6-b550-92b6c60a3048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{3} = \\frac{1}{6}x^{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8996977-d1af-5583-8f78-5ac2870cf12c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{3} = 3x^{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099453} :answer-field/id #uuid "238b4ed3-3283-5aaf-a6ab-0b987124f5dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c894685-37ff-5d18-b9c8-6edb0d037176" :question/math-academy-id "q-4019" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2x}{y^{2}}$ that satisfies $y(2) = \\sqrt[3]{13}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Separate and integrate:\n$$y^2\\,dy=2x\\,dx,\\qquad \\frac{y^3}{3}=x^2+C.$$\nWrite $y^3=3x^2+K$ and apply $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}$:\n$$13=12+K,\\qquad K=1.$$\nTherefore $y^3=3x^2+1$, or $y=\\sqrt[3]{3x^2+1}$."}]}
